proof.text:
Check the chain equation on the source coprojections. Abbreviate $\gamma_{C^p,D^q}$ by $\gamma_{p,q}$, and let $d_{\rm tot}$ and $d'_{\rm tot}$ be the two total differentials. For $p+q=n$, additivity from step 2 and the total differential formula give
\[
\begin{aligned}
\theta^{n+1}S(d_{\rm tot}^n)S(\iota_{p,q})
&=\iota'_{p+1,q}\gamma_{p+1,q}S(d_C^p\otimes1)
 +(-1)^p\iota'_{p,q+1}\gamma_{p,q+1}S(1\otimes d_D^q)\\
&=\iota'_{p+1,q}(S(d_C^p)\otimes1)\gamma_{p,q}
 +(-1)^p\iota'_{p,q+1}(1\otimes S(d_D^q))\gamma_{p,q}\\
&=d_{\rm tot}'{}^n\iota'_{p,q}\gamma_{p,q}
=d_{\rm tot}'{}^n\theta^n S(\iota_{p,q}).
\end{aligned}
\]
The middle equality is ordinary tensor naturality from step 1, separately for $(d_C^p,1)$ and $(1,d_D^q)$. Joint determination from step 3 proves the chain equation. Each $\theta^n$ is invertible, and multiplying this equation by the degreewise inverses proves that they too form a chain map. Hence $\theta$ is a chain isomorphism.
